    "slug": "irs-form-1095-b",
    "title": "CHAMPVA and IRS Form 1095-B: Do You Need It and How to Get It",
    "primary_question": "What is IRS Form 1095-B for CHAMPVA, do I need it, and how can I get a copy?",
    "summary": "CHAMPVA Form 1095-B documents health coverage for a tax year. It is no longer required for federal tax filing, but you may need it for state taxes or records; CHAMPVA family members currently request a paper copy from VA by phone.",
    "body_markdown": "\n## Quick answer\n\n**IRS Form 1095-B is proof of health coverage.** For CHAMPVA beneficiaries, it documents CHAMPVA coverage for the tax year, and the IRS specifically lists CHAMPVA as a government-sponsored form of minimum essential coverage.\n\nYou do **not** need to attach or file Form 1095-B with your federal income tax return. VA says you may still need a copy for a state tax filing or for your personal records.\n\nStarting in 2026, VA stopped automatically mailing 1095-B forms. As of September 20, 2026, a CHAMPVA family member who needs the form should request a **paper copy** by calling CHAMPVA at **800-733-8387 (TTY: 711)**, Monday through Friday, **8:00 a.m. to 7:30 p.m. ET**.\n\n*Last researched: September 20, 2026.*\n\n## What Form 1095-B tells you\n\nThe IRS uses Form 1095-B to report information about people who had **minimum essential coverage**. In the IRS instructions for Forms 1094-B and 1095-B, CHAMPVA is specifically listed among the Department of Veterans Affairs programs that count as government-sponsored minimum essential coverage.\n\nVA describes the CHAMPVA 1095-B as proof that you had CHAMPVA health coverage during the most recent tax year. The form is about health-coverage reporting; it does not replace your CHAMPVA ID card or enrollment records.\n\nYou can review the current rules in the [IRS instructions for Forms 1094-B and 1095-B](https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i109495b) and VA's [CHAMPVA care guidance](https://www.va.gov/resources/champva-care/).\n\n## Do you need Form 1095-B to file taxes?\n\n### Federal taxes\n\nVA says you **no longer need to file Form 1095-B with your federal taxes**. If you are preparing a federal return, the fact that VA did not automatically mail the form does not mean your CHAMPVA coverage changed.\n\n### State taxes\n\nVA says you **may still need the form for state tax filing**. State requirements are not uniform, so check the current instructions from your state tax agency or ask your tax preparer whether a 1095-B or another proof-of-coverage document is required.\n\n### Personal records\n\nYou can also request the form simply to keep documentation of your health coverage for the year.\n\n## How a CHAMPVA family member requests a copy\n\nVA's current family-member process is by phone:\n\n1. Call **CHAMPVA at 800-733-8387 (TTY: 711)**.\n2. Call Monday through Friday, **8:00 a.m. to 7:30 p.m. ET**.\n3. Tell the representative that you are a CHAMPVA family member and need your **IRS Form 1095-B**.\n4. Follow VA's identity-verification and mailing instructions for the paper copy.\n\nVA stopped automatically mailing these forms in 2026, so receiving CHAMPVA benefits by itself does not trigger an automatic 1095-B mailing.\n\nIf you need a current CHAMPVA contact for another issue, see [CHAMPVA Contacts, Mailing Addresses, and Official Forms](https://valorwell.org/resources/champva/contacts-addresses-forms).\n\n## Can CHAMPVA family members download the form online?\n\nNot yet under VA's current family-member instructions as of September 20, 2026. VA currently tells CHAMPVA family members to request a paper copy by phone.\n\nVA announced in January 2026 that it plans to make electronic 1095-B access available to family members through a VA.gov account **by January 2027**. That is a future rollout date, so use the current phone-request process unless VA has updated its instructions when you need the form.\n\nVeterans using VA health care have a different 1095-B process and can already access their form electronically. Do not assume the Veteran download workflow is the current CHAMPVA family-member workflow.\n\n## What changed in 2026?\n\nThe important change is **delivery**, not CHAMPVA eligibility or benefits. VA stopped automatically mailing Form 1095-B and moved to a request-based process for family members.\n\nVA's January 2026 announcement specifically says the change does **not** affect VA health care coverage or benefits. It only changes how the form is received.\n\n## If you need proof of CHAMPVA coverage for a provider\n\nForm 1095-B is a tax-related coverage statement, not the document a provider usually needs for eligibility or billing. If a medical office is asking for CHAMPVA identification or payer details, see [CHAMPVA Welcome Letter, ID Card, and Payer Information](https://valorwell.org/resources/champva/id-card-payer-information).\n\n## A quick tax-form distinction\n\nForm **1095-B** reports minimum essential health coverage. It is different from Form **1095-A**, which is generally associated with Health Insurance Marketplace coverage, and Form **1095-C**, which is associated with certain employer-sponsored coverage.\n\nIf a tax preparer or state agency asks for a specific form, confirm which form they actually need before requesting records.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nFor a CHAMPVA beneficiary, Form 1095-B is evidence of health coverage. You do not need to file it with your federal income tax return, but you may need it for a state filing or your records. VA no longer mails it automatically. Under the current CHAMPVA family-member process, request a paper copy by calling **800-733-8387 (TTY: 711)** during CHAMPVA customer-service hours.\n      ",
